A||N||5||Ozzy||Exhaust Problems again!!||||9||
Z||000000||Ozzy||05-11-2000||12:52 AM||||Well, the V-6 exhaust is beginning to reveal its true colors! I had it working well but I noticed a leak starting. I took a hose and listened around the Y pipe and front manifold. There was a leak starting in the middle of the Y pipe so I pulled it and had it welded by my local muffler shop. They did a real clean job too. (Most repairs I have seen look like bird sh*t on the exhaust piping!) So I re-installed it tonight and notice that the used front manifold I just recently placed is now cracked as well! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>So I guess I need pull it off again. Man that front manifold is a pain in the A$$!<P>Looking closer at how the manifolds line up to the crossover pipe, it is easy to see why these things break. Not to mention that there seems to be the weight of the cat and front part of the exhaust system bouncing up and down on weak manifolds.<P>Other than buying $600.00 headers, (Don't want to spend that money on it right now)has anyone come up with some good tweaks on the V-6 exhaust to extend it's life?? What about a support bracket from the bell housing to the crossover pipe??<P><BR>Thoughts anyone??||||reg||9
Z||000001||Pretender||05-11-2000||01:43 AM||||Pulling the factory manifold isnt that hard, front or rear, but the problem lies in the bolts, make sure you spray then down ALOT! the cheapest and probably the best way since you don't want to spend the time and money on a set of headders PORT! once the manifolds are off you will see what needs ported and if you have an air grinder/dremel it'll only take you about an hour or so. DO NOT repeat DO-NOT buy an ocelot exhaust system. better off going with the stock unit, thicker gauge piping and a better sounding muffler too. I've learned the hard way from my exhaust systems. hope i helped you out a little..||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ozzy||05-11-2000||01:54 AM||||Thnx Pretender...<P>I ported the manifolds when I went thru this recently. I actually had no problems with studs breaking. It is just getting to them that is a pain!!! I might pull the intake manifold next time. The extra room and access from the top would help. I also placed new studs after removing the old ones based on what I have learned here.<P>The rest of the exhaust system is not a problem other than some vibration. It is just the poor fit of the x-over pipe to the manifolds and what I see as stress loading. These manifolds would even be better if they were made from heavier material.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||05-12-2000||03:40 AM||||You don't have to take off the intake manifolds. Just undo the rear cradle bolts. The engine cradle will then hinge forward and down, revealing....ta-da! The manifold bolts! Now it won't be much easier, some people event take off the trunklid to get them as well, thats okay too as it is easy to realign the lid when you are done. You can lower it down as much as you want or as much as you are comfortable. The dogbone will ultimately stop it from going any further. Good luck!||||reg||6
Z||000004||DRH||05-12-2000||04:27 PM||||I did mine from underneath with it on ramps. It helps to take off the heat shield on the lower firewall. Mine does have an aftermarket cat. convertor which may be smaller than the original. I'm not sure how much this will affect accessability. <P>The only bolt I had any problem with is the one behind the AC compressor. With exactly the right wrench you MIGHT be able to get it without moving the compressor back. I wound up taking the compressor bracket loose from the top where it bolts to the head and dropping the whole thing down a few inches.<P>BTW, the guy that welded my manifold said the problem is in the design. The pipes coming from the head are too short. They don't leave enough room for the larger pipe to expand and contract properly when heated and cooled. (Not a Fiero guy, he does mostly custom fabrication for stock cars and street rods. He did seem to know what he was talking about though.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ozzy||05-13-2000||02:27 AM||||DRH...I did not remove the AC compressor but it WAS a real pain. I took one of those old cheap bicycle tool kit wrenches (thin and no angle) and ground a concave groove the same diam. as the manifols tubing. This gave me the extra 1/8" (approx.) rotation to get the next bite on that bolt. Slow but it worked.<P>WKD...I read the TSB on this procedure last night. I thought it described dropping the front cradle bolts swinging the front manifold towards the floor and accesing from underneath. Maybe I misunderstood. What else do you need to disconnect and how much room do you gain. Is the extra time and hassle dropping the cradle worth it?<P><BR>Ozzy<P>||||reg||1
